ADDART({id:"kyoto",t:"Kyoto",c:"Countries and Places",i:"⛩️",w:"Kyoto",r:["japancountry","japan","buddh","shinto","climchange"],
p:[["Japan’s old capital","Kyoto was the capital of Japan and home of its emperors for more than a thousand years."],["Temples and shrines","It has around 1,600 Buddhist temples and 400 Shinto shrines."],["Living tradition","Tea houses, gardens and geisha districts keep old traditions alive."]],
l:"Kyoto is a city in western Japan, on the island of Honshu, with about 1.5 million people. It was founded in 794 as Heian-kyō, ‘the capital of peace and tranquility’, and served as Japan’s imperial capital until 1868, when the emperor moved to Tokyo. During those centuries it was the center of Japanese culture, religion and the arts. Kyoto has around 1,600 Buddhist temples and 400 Shinto shrines, as well as palaces, gardens and traditional wooden houses, and 17 of its sites are part of a UNESCO World Heritage listing. Famous sights include the Golden Pavilion, the thousands of red gates at Fushimi Inari Shrine and the bamboo groves of Arashiyama. The 1997 Kyoto Protocol on climate change was agreed there.",
s:[["The capital of peace","Heian-kyō was laid out on a grid pattern copied from Chang’an, the capital of Tang dynasty China, with a wide central avenue leading to the imperial palace. During the Heian period, court life flourished, and women writers such as Murasaki Shikibu, author of The Tale of Genji, created masterpieces of literature."],["Temples, shrines and gardens","Kyoto’s Buddhist temples include Kinkaku-ji, the Golden Pavilion, covered in gold leaf, and Kiyomizu-dera, whose wooden stage juts out from a hillside. Fushimi Inari Shrine, dedicated to the Shinto god of rice, has thousands of vermilion torii gates winding up a mountain. The rock garden of Ryōan-ji is a famous example of Zen design."],["Traditions","Kyoto is a center of traditional arts such as the tea ceremony, flower arranging, Noh theater, silk weaving and pottery. In the Gion district, geiko and maiko, as Kyoto’s geisha and apprentice geisha are called, entertain guests with dance, music and conversation. The Gion Matsuri festival in July dates back more than 1,100 years."],["Spared in war","Kyoto was largely spared bombing in the Second World War. It was on the list of possible targets for the atomic bomb, but it was removed, reportedly because US Secretary of War Henry Stimson knew of its cultural importance. As a result, much of its historic architecture survives, and today it is one of the world’s most visited cities."]],
k:[["Country","Japan"],["Population","About 1.5 million"],["Founded","794, as Heian-kyō"],["Imperial capital","794–1868"],["Temples and shrines","About 1,600 and 400"]],
d:[["794","Heian-kyō is founded as the capital"],["869","The Gion Matsuri festival begins"],["1397","The Golden Pavilion is built"],["1868","The emperor moves to Tokyo"],["1994","Historic monuments listed by UNESCO"],["1997","The Kyoto Protocol is agreed"]],
s2:[["The Golden Pavilion","Kinkaku-ji, the Golden Pavilion, was built in 1397 as a villa for a retired shogun and later became a Zen temple. Its upper floors are covered in gold leaf, reflected in the pond in front. In 1950, a young monk set fire to it, and it burned to the ground. The pavilion was rebuilt in 1955, and its gold leaf was renewed in the 1980s."],["Fushimi Inari","Fushimi Inari Shrine is famous for its tunnels of vermilion torii gates, thousands of them, climbing the forested slopes of Mount Inari. Each gate was donated by a person or business, whose name is written on it. Statues of foxes, believed to be the messengers of the god Inari, guard the shrine."]],
sp:["The Kyoto Protocol","In December 1997, countries meeting in Kyoto agreed the Kyoto Protocol, the first international treaty in which developed countries committed to cut their greenhouse gas emissions. It came into force in 2005 and was later followed by the Paris Agreement of 2015."],
g:[["Torii","A gate marking the entrance to a Shinto shrine"],["Maiko","An apprentice geisha in Kyoto"],["Zen","A school of Buddhism focused on meditation"],["Heian","The period of Japanese history from 794 to 1185"]],
f:["Kyoto was Japan’s capital for over 1,000 years.","Kyoto has around 1,600 Buddhist temples.","The Golden Pavilion was rebuilt after being burned in 1950."],
fun:"The name Kyoto simply means ‘capital city’; Tokyo means ‘eastern capital’.",
tl:"Ang Kyoto ang dating kabisera ng Hapon at tahanan ng mga emperador nang mahigit isang libong taon. May mga 1,600 templong Budista at 400 dambanang Shinto ito, at buhay pa rito ang mga lumang tradisyon."});

ADDART({id:"hongkong",t:"Hong Kong",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌃",w:"Hong Kong",r:["chinacountry","opium","modernchina","skyscrapers","trade"],
p:[["A harbor city","Hong Kong is a dense, mountainous city around one of the world’s finest natural harbors."],["British and Chinese","It was a British colony for over 150 years before returning to China in 1997."],["A financial center","It is one of Asia’s leading centers of finance and trade."]],
l:"Hong Kong is a special administrative region of China on the southern coast, made up of Hong Kong Island, the Kowloon Peninsula, the New Territories and over 260 islands. About 7.5 million people live there, mostly crowded onto a small amount of flat land between steep, green mountains, making it one of the most densely populated places on Earth. Britain took Hong Kong Island in 1842 after the First Opium War and later added Kowloon and the leased New Territories. In 1997, Hong Kong returned to China under the principle of ‘one country, two systems’, which promised it a high degree of autonomy for 50 years. It is a major global center of finance, trade and shipping, famous for its skyline, harbor, food and film industry.",
s:[["From fishing villages to colony","Before the British arrived, the area was home to fishing and farming villages. After the First Opium War, China ceded Hong Kong Island to Britain in the Treaty of Nanking in 1842. Kowloon was added in 1860, and in 1898 the New Territories were leased to Britain for 99 years. Hong Kong grew into a busy free port."],["War and growth","Japan occupied Hong Kong from December 1941 to 1945. After the Second World War and the Chinese Civil War, hundreds of thousands of people fled from mainland China to Hong Kong, bringing skills and money. Factories making textiles, toys and electronics boomed, and later Hong Kong became a center of banking and services."],["Return to China","As the lease on the New Territories was due to end, Britain and China agreed in 1984 that all of Hong Kong would return to China. On 1 July 1997, it became a special administrative region with its own legal system, currency and border controls. In 2019, large protests took place, and in 2020 China introduced a national security law, which critics say has reduced freedoms and supporters say restored stability."],["Life in Hong Kong","Hong Kong has more tall buildings than almost any other city. Most people live in high-rise apartments and travel by an efficient metro, buses, trams and ferries. Its food culture is famous, especially dim sum. Cantonese is the main language, with English and Mandarin also widely used, and its films, including martial arts movies, became popular worldwide."]],
k:[["Country","China (special administrative region)"],["Population","About 7.5 million"],["Name means","‘Fragrant harbor’"],["British rule","1842–1997"],["Main language","Cantonese"]],
d:[["1842","Hong Kong Island ceded to Britain"],["1860","Kowloon added"],["1898","The New Territories leased for 99 years"],["1941–45","Japanese occupation"],["1997","Returned to China"],["2020","National security law introduced"]],
s2:[["The Star Ferry","Since 1888, the green-and-white Star Ferry boats have carried passengers across Victoria Harbour between Kowloon and Hong Kong Island. The short trip offers one of the most famous views of the city’s skyline and remains popular with residents and visitors."],["The Peak Tram","The Peak Tram, opened in 1888, climbs steeply up Victoria Peak, the highest point on Hong Kong Island. From the top, visitors look down over the skyscrapers and the harbor. The city’s nightly ‘Symphony of Lights’ show illuminates the skyline with lasers and lights."]],
sp:["Dim sum","Dim sum, meaning ‘touch the heart’, are small dishes such as dumplings, buns and rice rolls, served in bamboo steamers with tea. Going for ‘yum cha’, or drinking tea, with family on weekend mornings is a Hong Kong tradition."],
g:[["Special administrative region","A region of China with its own government and laws"],["Colony","A territory ruled by another country"],["Lease","An agreement to use land for a set time"],["Cantonese","A Chinese language spoken in Hong Kong and southern China"]],
f:["Hong Kong means ‘fragrant harbor’.","It has over 260 islands.","The Star Ferry has crossed Victoria Harbour since 1888."],
fun:"Hong Kong’s Central–Mid-Levels escalator is the world’s longest outdoor covered escalator system, about 800 meters long.",
tl:"Ang Hong Kong ay masikip at bulubunduking lungsod sa paligid ng isang mahusay na daungan. Naging kolonya ito ng Britanya nang mahigit 150 taon bago ibinalik sa Tsina noong 1997, at isa itong sentro ng pananalapi sa Asya."});

ADDART({id:"bangkok",t:"Bangkok",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🛕",w:"Bangkok",r:["thailandcountry","buddh","megacities","rivers","tourism"],
p:[["Thailand’s capital","Bangkok is the capital and largest city of Thailand, on the Chao Phraya River."],["Temples and palaces","Its golden temples and the Grand Palace attract millions of visitors."],["The longest name","Its full ceremonial name is one of the longest place names in the world."]],
l:"Bangkok is the capital and by far the largest city of Thailand, home to over 10 million people in its metropolitan area. It lies on the Chao Phraya River near the Gulf of Thailand. Bangkok has been the capital since 1782, when King Rama I, founder of the Chakri dynasty that still reigns, built a new royal city there. Once crossed by canals, it was called the ‘Venice of the East’. Today it is a huge, lively city of skyscrapers, markets, street food and traffic, and one of the most visited cities in the world. Its landmarks include the Grand Palace, Wat Phra Kaew with the Emerald Buddha, Wat Pho with its giant reclining Buddha, and Wat Arun on the riverbank.",
s:[["A royal capital","After the old capital of Ayutthaya was destroyed by a Burmese army in 1767, the capital moved to Thonburi, on the river’s west bank. In 1782, King Rama I moved it across the river to Bangkok and built the Grand Palace. The Thai name for the city, Krung Thep, means ‘city of angels’."],["Temples","Bangkok has hundreds of Buddhist temples, called wat. Wat Phra Kaew, inside the Grand Palace, houses the Emerald Buddha, Thailand’s most sacred image, carved from green jade. Wat Pho has a reclining Buddha 46 meters long covered in gold leaf, and is a famous school of Thai massage. Wat Arun, the Temple of Dawn, is decorated with colorful porcelain."],["Canals and the river","Bangkok was once a city of canals, called khlongs, where people traveled and traded by boat. Many canals were filled in to build roads in the 20th century, but some remain, along with floating markets on the city’s outskirts. River boats still carry commuters along the Chao Phraya."],["Modern Bangkok","Bangkok grew rapidly from the 1960s, becoming the center of Thailand’s economy. The elevated Skytrain opened in 1999, followed by an underground metro, to ease its famous traffic jams. The city is built on soft, low-lying land and is slowly sinking, which, along with rising seas, makes it vulnerable to flooding."]],
k:[["Country","Thailand"],["Population","Over 10 million (metro area)"],["River","Chao Phraya"],["Capital since","1782"],["Thai name","Krung Thep, ‘city of angels’"]],
d:[["1767","Ayutthaya is destroyed; the capital moves to Thonburi"],["1782","King Rama I founds Bangkok as the capital"],["1782","The Grand Palace is begun"],["1932","Thailand becomes a constitutional monarchy"],["1999","The Skytrain opens"],["2011","Severe floods hit Bangkok"]],
s2:[["A very long name","Bangkok’s full ceremonial name in Thai is a long poetic phrase, starting ‘Krung Thep Maha Nakhon’, that describes it as the city of angels, the great city of immortals and the residence of the Emerald Buddha. It is often listed as the longest place name in the world. Thais usually just say Krung Thep."],["Street food","Bangkok is famous for its street food: stalls and carts sell dishes such as pad thai, green curry, som tam papaya salad, grilled meats and mango with sticky rice. Areas such as Yaowarat, Bangkok’s Chinatown, come alive at night with food sellers."]],
sp:["The Emerald Buddha","The Emerald Buddha is a small statue, about 66 centimeters tall, carved from a single piece of green jade. Thailand’s king changes its golden costume three times a year, for the hot, rainy and cool seasons, in a royal ceremony."],
g:[["Wat","A Buddhist temple in Thailand"],["Khlong","A canal in Thailand"],["Chakri dynasty","The royal family that has ruled Thailand since 1782"],["Tuk-tuk","A motorized three-wheeled taxi"]],
f:["Bangkok’s Thai name, Krung Thep, means ‘city of angels’.","Wat Pho’s reclining Buddha is 46 meters long.","Bangkok has been Thailand’s capital since 1782."],
fun:"The name Bangkok probably comes from an old village whose name referred to a kind of olive or plum tree.",
tl:"Ang Bangkok ang kabisera at pinakamalaking lungsod ng Thailand, sa tabi ng Ilog Chao Phraya. Tanyag ang mga ginintuang templo at ang Grand Palace nito, at isa sa pinakamahahabang pangalan ng lugar sa mundo ang buong pangalang seremonyal nito."});

ADDART({id:"toronto",t:"Toronto",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🍁",w:"Toronto",r:["canadacountry","megacities","migration","skyscrapers","bball"],
p:[["Canada’s biggest city","Toronto is the largest city in Canada, on the shore of Lake Ontario."],["A city of newcomers","Nearly half its residents were born outside Canada."],["The CN Tower","Its famous tower was the world’s tallest freestanding structure for over 30 years."]],
l:"Toronto is the largest city in Canada and the capital of the province of Ontario, with about 2.8 million people in the city and over 6 million in its metropolitan area. It lies on the northwestern shore of Lake Ontario. The area was long home to Indigenous peoples, and its name probably comes from a Mohawk word meaning ‘where there are trees standing in the water’. Founded by the British as the town of York in 1793, it was renamed Toronto in 1834. It is Canada’s financial and business center and one of the most multicultural cities in the world, where nearly half of residents were born in another country. Its skyline is dominated by the CN Tower.",
s:[["Origins","For thousands of years, Indigenous peoples, including the Huron-Wendat, Haudenosaunee and Mississaugas of the Credit, lived and traveled in the area, which lay on a trade route between Lake Ontario and Lake Huron. In 1793, British colonial governor John Graves Simcoe founded the town of York there. American forces raided it during the War of 1812."],["Growth","Renamed Toronto in 1834, the city grew rapidly with railways, industry and immigration from Britain and Ireland, and later from Italy, Portugal, Greece, the Caribbean, China, South Asia and many other places. By the 1970s, it had overtaken Montreal as Canada’s largest city and business capital."],["A multicultural city","More than 200 ethnic origins are represented in Toronto, and over 180 languages are spoken. Neighborhoods such as Chinatown, Little Italy, Greektown, Little Portugal and Little India reflect its communities. The city’s motto is ‘Diversity Our Strength’."],["Culture and sport","The Toronto International Film Festival, founded in 1976, is one of the world’s most important film festivals. The city’s teams include the Toronto Maple Leafs ice hockey team, the Blue Jays, who won baseball’s World Series in 1992 and 1993, and the Raptors, who won the NBA basketball championship in 2019."]],
k:[["Country","Canada"],["Province","Ontario (capital)"],["Population","About 2.8 million (city)"],["Lake","Ontario"],["Landmark","CN Tower, 553 m"]],
d:[["1793","The town of York is founded"],["1813","American forces raid York"],["1834","Renamed Toronto"],["1976","The CN Tower is completed"],["1992–93","Blue Jays win the World Series twice"],["2019","Raptors win the NBA championship"]],
s2:[["The CN Tower","The CN Tower, completed in 1976, is 553 meters tall. Built as a communications tower, it was the world’s tallest freestanding structure until 2007. Visitors can stand on its glass floor 342 meters above the ground or, on the EdgeWalk, walk around the outside of the tower on a ledge while attached to a safety harness."],["The PATH","Beneath downtown Toronto lies the PATH, a network of underground walkways about 30 kilometers long, linking office towers, shops, restaurants and stations. It is one of the largest underground shopping complexes in the world and lets people avoid the city’s cold winters."]],
sp:["Niagara Falls nearby","About 130 kilometers from Toronto, on the border between Canada and the United States, lie Niagara Falls, where the Niagara River plunges over a cliff. The Horseshoe Falls, on the Canadian side, are the most powerful waterfall in North America by volume of water."],
g:[["Multicultural","Made up of many different cultures"],["Province","A large region with its own government in Canada"],["Freestanding","Standing on its own without support cables"],["Indigenous","The original inhabitants of a place"]],
f:["Nearly half of Toronto’s residents were born outside Canada.","The CN Tower is 553 meters tall.","Toronto was called York until 1834."],
fun:"The CN Tower is struck by lightning dozens of times a year, but its lightning rods keep it safe.",
tl:"Ang Toronto ang pinakamalaking lungsod sa Canada, sa pampang ng Lawa ng Ontario. Halos kalahati ng mga residente nito ay isinilang sa ibang bansa, at ang CN Tower nito ang pinakamataas na nakatayong istruktura sa mundo nang mahigit 30 taon."});

ADDART({id:"jerusalem",t:"Jerusalem",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🕍",w:"Jerusalem",r:["judaism","christ","islam","crusades","middleeast"],
p:[["A holy city","Jerusalem is sacred to Judaism, Christianity and Islam."],["Thousands of years old","It has been fought over, destroyed and rebuilt many times."],["A disputed city","Its status is one of the most contested issues in the Israeli–Palestinian conflict."]],
l:"Jerusalem is an ancient city in the hills between the Mediterranean Sea and the Dead Sea, with a history going back more than 3,000 years as a major city. It is holy to three religions: for Jews, it is the site of the ancient Temples and the city of King David; for Christians, it is where Jesus taught, was crucified and, they believe, rose from the dead; and for Muslims, it is the third-holiest city, from which the Prophet Muhammad is believed to have ascended to heaven. Its walled Old City contains the Western Wall, the Church of the Holy Sepulchre, the Dome of the Rock and al-Aqsa Mosque. Israel regards the whole city as its capital, while Palestinians seek East Jerusalem as the capital of a future state; most countries keep their embassies elsewhere.",
s:[["Ancient Jerusalem","According to the Bible, King David made Jerusalem his capital around 1000 BC, and his son Solomon built the First Temple. The Babylonians destroyed the city and the Temple in 587 BC. A Second Temple was built after the exile and later greatly expanded by King Herod. In AD 70, the Romans destroyed the Second Temple after a Jewish revolt."],["Christian and Muslim Jerusalem","The Roman emperor Constantine built the Church of the Holy Sepulchre in the 4th century over the site where Christians believe Jesus was crucified and buried. Muslim armies took the city in 637 or 638, and the Dome of the Rock was completed in 691–692 on the Temple Mount, known to Muslims as the Haram al-Sharif. Crusaders captured Jerusalem in 1099, and Saladin retook it in 1187."],["Ottoman and British rule","The Ottoman Empire ruled Jerusalem from 1517 to 1917; Sultan Suleiman the Magnificent built the walls that still surround the Old City. Britain took the city in 1917 and governed it under a League of Nations mandate. In the 1948 war, the city was divided between Israel in the west and Jordan in the east, including the Old City."],["A contested city","Israel captured East Jerusalem in the 1967 war and later declared the whole city its capital, a move not recognized by most of the international community. Palestinians, many of whom live in East Jerusalem, regard it as the capital of a future Palestinian state. Access to holy sites and the city’s future remain highly sensitive issues."]],
k:[["Location","Between the Mediterranean and the Dead Sea"],["Holy to","Judaism, Christianity and Islam"],["Old City walls","Built in the 1530s"],["Old City area","About 0.9 km²"],["UNESCO site","Old City, since 1981"]],
d:[["c.1000 BC","King David makes Jerusalem his capital"],["587 BC","The Babylonians destroy the First Temple"],["AD 70","The Romans destroy the Second Temple"],["691–692","The Dome of the Rock is completed"],["1099","The Crusaders capture the city"],["1517–1917","Ottoman rule"],["1967","Israel captures East Jerusalem"]],
s2:[["The four quarters","The Old City is traditionally divided into four quarters: the Muslim, Christian, Jewish and Armenian Quarters. Within its walls, less than a square kilometer in area, are some of the holiest places in the world, narrow stone streets, markets and homes. Eight gates lead through the walls."],["The Western Wall","The Western Wall is part of a retaining wall built by King Herod to support the platform of the Second Temple. It is the holiest place where Jews are permitted to pray, and visitors place written prayers in the cracks between its stones. Above it, on the same platform, stand the Dome of the Rock and al-Aqsa Mosque."]],
sp:["The Dome of the Rock","The Dome of the Rock, with its gleaming golden dome and blue tiles, is one of the oldest surviving Islamic buildings, completed in 691–692. It covers a rock sacred to both Muslims, who believe Muhammad ascended to heaven from there, and Jews, who associate it with the site of the ancient Temple."],
g:[["Temple Mount","The hill where the Jewish Temples stood, called Haram al-Sharif by Muslims"],["Pilgrimage","A journey to a holy place"],["Old City","The walled historic center of Jerusalem"],["Mandate","Authority given to a country to govern a territory"]],
f:["Jerusalem’s Old City is less than one square kilometer.","The Old City walls were built by the Ottoman sultan Suleiman the Magnificent.","Jerusalem is holy to three major religions."],
fun:"Jerusalem’s buildings are, by law, faced with local pale limestone, called Jerusalem stone, which glows golden in the sunset.",
tl:"Ang Jerusalem ay banal na lungsod para sa Hudaismo, Kristiyanismo at Islam. Ilang ulit itong pinag-agawan, winasak at muling itinayo, at hanggang ngayon ay pinagtatalunan ang katayuan nito."});

ADDART({id:"magnacarta",t:"Magna Carta",c:"History",i:"📜",w:"Magna Carta",r:["law","democ","hrights","medieurope","ukcountry"],
p:[["The Great Charter","Magna Carta was an agreement made in England in 1215 between King John and rebel barons."],["No one above the law","It set out the idea that even the king must obey the law."],["A lasting symbol","It inspired later ideas of rights and constitutions around the world."]],
l:"Magna Carta, Latin for ‘Great Charter’, is a document agreed by King John of England at Runnymede, near Windsor, on 15 June 1215, to make peace with a group of rebellious barons. It promised to protect church rights, limit taxes, protect barons from illegal imprisonment and guarantee swift justice. Although the pope annulled it within weeks and war broke out, it was reissued in revised forms in 1216, 1217 and 1225, and became part of English law. Over the centuries, Magna Carta came to symbolize the principle that rulers are subject to the law and that people have rights that must be respected, inspiring the United States Constitution and the Universal Declaration of Human Rights.",
s:[["Why it was made","King John was unpopular: he had lost lands in France, raised heavy taxes to pay for wars and quarreled with the Church. In 1215, a group of barons rebelled and captured London. To end the crisis, John met them at Runnymede, a meadow by the River Thames, and agreed to the charter."],["What it said","Magna Carta has 63 clauses, written in Latin. Many dealt with medieval concerns such as inheritance, feudal payments and fish weirs on rivers. The most famous, clause 39, said that no free man could be imprisoned, stripped of his rights or punished except by the lawful judgment of his equals or by the law of the land."],["Reissued and remembered","The 1215 charter quickly failed, but after John’s death, his son’s advisers reissued it to win support. The 1225 version became law. Most of its clauses have since been repealed, but three remain part of English law, including the right to justice and a fair trial."],["A symbol of liberty","In the 17th century, English lawyers used Magna Carta to argue against kings who claimed absolute power. American colonists cited it in their struggle for independence, and its ideas echo in the US Bill of Rights. In 1948, Eleanor Roosevelt hoped the Universal Declaration of Human Rights would become ‘the international Magna Carta for all’."]],
k:[["Agreed","15 June 1215"],["Place","Runnymede, England"],["King","John"],["Clauses","63"],["Original copies surviving","Four from 1215"]],
d:[["1199","John becomes king of England"],["1215","Magna Carta is agreed at Runnymede"],["1215","The pope annuls it; war breaks out"],["1225","A revised version becomes law"],["1791","The US Bill of Rights draws on its ideas"],["1948","Universal Declaration of Human Rights"]],
s2:[["The surviving copies","Copies of Magna Carta were written by scribes and sent around the kingdom. Four copies of the 1215 version survive: two in the British Library in London, one in Lincoln Castle and one in Salisbury Cathedral. One copy of the 1297 version was sold in 2007 for over 21 million US dollars and is displayed in Washington, D.C."],["The memorial at Runnymede","At Runnymede, a memorial built by the American Bar Association in 1957 honors Magna Carta as a symbol of freedom under the law. Nearby is a memorial to US President John F. Kennedy, on land given to the United States."]],
sp:["Clause 39","Clause 39 stated that no free man should be seized, imprisoned, stripped of his rights or possessions, outlawed, exiled or otherwise ruined, except by the lawful judgment of his peers or by the law of the land. At the time, ‘free men’ were a minority, but the idea grew into the right to a fair trial for everyone."],
g:[["Charter","A written grant of rights or privileges"],["Baron","A powerful noble who held land from the king"],["Clause","A section of a legal document"],["Annul","To declare something no longer valid"]],
f:["Magna Carta was written in Latin.","Four original copies from 1215 survive.","Three of its clauses are still part of English law."],
fun:"King John did not sign Magna Carta; it was sealed with his royal seal.",
tl:"Ang Magna Carta ay kasunduang ginawa sa Inglatera noong 1215 sa pagitan ni Haring John at ng mga baron na naghimagsik. Itinakda nito na kahit ang hari ay dapat sumunod sa batas, at nagbigay-inspirasyon ito sa mga konstitusyon sa buong mundo."});

ADDART({id:"babylon",t:"Babylon",c:"History",i:"🦁",w:"Babylon",r:["mesop","persia","alexander","writing","judaism"],
p:[["A great ancient city","Babylon was one of the largest and most famous cities of the ancient world, in Mesopotamia."],["Laws and learning","King Hammurabi’s law code and Babylonian astronomy shaped later civilizations."],["Gates and gardens","Nebuchadnezzar II built the blue Ishtar Gate and, legend says, the Hanging Gardens."]],
l:"Babylon was an ancient city on the Euphrates River in Mesopotamia, about 85 kilometers south of modern Baghdad in Iraq. It rose to power under King Hammurabi in the 18th century BC, who issued one of the earliest written law codes, and reached its greatest splendor under King Nebuchadnezzar II in the 6th century BC, when it may have been the largest city in the world. Its monuments included massive walls, the glazed-brick Ishtar Gate, a great ziggurat that may have inspired the biblical story of the Tower of Babel, and, according to Greek writers, the Hanging Gardens, one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World. Babylonian scholars made major advances in astronomy and mathematics. Its ruins are a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
s:[["Hammurabi’s Babylon","Hammurabi ruled from about 1792 to 1750 BC and turned Babylon from a small city into the capital of an empire covering much of Mesopotamia. His law code, carved on a tall stone pillar, contains 282 laws on trade, property, family and crime, including the principle of ‘an eye for an eye’. The pillar is now in the Louvre in Paris."],["The Neo-Babylonian Empire","After centuries under other powers, Babylon rose again in the 7th century BC. Nebuchadnezzar II, who ruled from 605 to 562 BC, conquered a large empire, including Jerusalem, whose people were taken into exile in Babylon. He rebuilt the city on a grand scale, with temples, palaces and walls wide enough, it was said, for chariots to pass on top."],["Science and the stars","Babylonian astronomers recorded the movements of the planets and the moon for centuries on clay tablets and could predict eclipses. Their mathematics used a base-60 number system, which is why we still divide an hour into 60 minutes and a circle into 360 degrees. They also developed the zodiac."],["Fall and rediscovery","In 539 BC, Cyrus the Great of Persia captured Babylon. Alexander the Great planned to make it his capital and died there in 323 BC. The city gradually declined and was abandoned. Excavations by German archaeologists from 1899 uncovered its ruins, and the Ishtar Gate was reconstructed in a museum in Berlin."]],
k:[["Location","On the Euphrates, Iraq"],["Famous king","Hammurabi"],["Greatest builder","Nebuchadnezzar II"],["Famous gate","The Ishtar Gate"],["UNESCO site","Since 2019"]],
d:[["c.1792–1750 BC","Reign of Hammurabi"],["605–562 BC","Reign of Nebuchadnezzar II"],["587 BC","Jerusalem destroyed; Jews exiled to Babylon"],["539 BC","Cyrus the Great of Persia captures Babylon"],["323 BC","Alexander the Great dies in Babylon"],["1899","German excavations begin"]],
s2:[["The Ishtar Gate","The Ishtar Gate, built around 575 BC, was one of eight gates into the inner city. It was covered in shining blue glazed bricks decorated with rows of golden lions, bulls and dragons, and led to a grand Processional Way. Its reconstructed front now stands in the Pergamon Museum in Berlin."],["Did the Hanging Gardens exist?","Greek writers described terraced gardens planted with trees and watered by machines, built by a Babylonian king for his homesick queen. However, no Babylonian records mention them, and archaeologists have not found clear remains. Some scholars think the gardens were actually in Nineveh, built by an Assyrian king."]],
sp:["Counting in sixties","The Babylonians counted in groups of 60, probably because 60 can be divided evenly in many ways: by 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 10, 12, 15, 20 and 30. This system survives today in how we measure time in hours, minutes and seconds, and angles in degrees."],
g:[["Mesopotamia","The ancient land between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers"],["Ziggurat","A stepped temple tower of ancient Mesopotamia"],["Law code","A written collection of laws"],["Exile","Being forced to live away from one’s home country"]],
f:["Hammurabi’s law code contains 282 laws.","We divide an hour into 60 minutes because of Babylonian mathematics.","The Ishtar Gate was decorated with lions, bulls and dragons."],
fun:"Babylonian scribes wrote down the oldest known recipes, on clay tablets about 3,700 years ago.",
tl:"Ang Babylon ay isa sa pinakamalalaki at pinakatanyag na lungsod noong sinaunang panahon, sa Mesopotamia. Kilala ito sa kodigo ng batas ni Hammurabi, sa astronomiya at sa asul na Ishtar Gate ni Nebuchadnezzar II."});

ADDART({id:"minoans",t:"The Minoans",c:"History",i:"🐂",w:"Minoan civilization",r:["greece","greececountry","phoenicians","volc","writing"],
p:[["Europe’s first great civilization","The Minoans built a rich Bronze Age civilization on the Greek island of Crete."],["Palaces and paintings","They built great palaces such as Knossos, decorated with bright frescoes."],["A lost script","Their writing, Linear A, has never been deciphered."]],
l:"The Minoans were a Bronze Age civilization on the island of Crete, in the Aegean Sea, that flourished from about 3000 to 1450 BC and is often called the first advanced civilization in Europe. They were skilled seafarers and traders who exchanged goods with Egypt, the Near East and other Aegean islands. They built large palace complexes, the greatest at Knossos, with many rooms, staircases, light wells and drainage systems, decorated with lively wall paintings of animals, plants, sea creatures and people, including the famous bull-leaping scenes. Their writing, Linear A, remains undeciphered. The name Minoan comes from the legendary King Minos and was given by the archaeologist Arthur Evans, who excavated Knossos from 1900.",
s:[["Palaces","Minoan palaces, at Knossos, Phaistos, Malia and elsewhere, were centers of government, religion, storage and craft production, built around large central courtyards. Knossos had over a thousand rooms, with storerooms holding huge clay jars of oil and grain. Its complex layout may have inspired the Greek legend of the labyrinth."],["Art and life","Minoan frescoes show dolphins, lilies, monkeys, processions and athletes leaping over charging bulls. Their pottery was decorated with octopuses and plants. Women appear prominently in Minoan art, in religious ceremonies and public events. The Minoans worshipped goddesses and used sacred symbols such as the double axe."],["Writing","The Minoans used a script now called Linear A, written on clay tablets, which has never been deciphered, so their language is unknown. After the Mycenaeans from mainland Greece took control of Crete, scribes used a related script, Linear B, which Michael Ventris deciphered in 1952 as an early form of Greek."],["Decline","Around 1600 BC, the volcano on the island of Thera, now Santorini, erupted in one of the largest eruptions in human history, burying the Minoan town of Akrotiri. Around 1450 BC, many Minoan sites were destroyed, and the Mycenaeans took over Knossos. Historians still debate what caused the Minoans’ decline."]],
k:[["Location","Crete, Greece"],["Period","About 3000–1450 BC"],["Greatest palace","Knossos"],["Script","Linear A (undeciphered)"],["Named after","Legendary King Minos"]],
d:[["c.3000 BC","Minoan civilization begins on Crete"],["c.1900 BC","The first great palaces are built"],["c.1600 BC","The volcano on Thera erupts"],["c.1450 BC","Many Minoan sites are destroyed"],["1900","Arthur Evans begins excavating Knossos"],["1952","Linear B is deciphered"]],
s2:[["The minotaur","Greek legend told of the Minotaur, a monster half man and half bull, kept by King Minos in a labyrinth at Knossos and fed with young Athenians, until the hero Theseus killed it with the help of Minos’s daughter Ariadne, who gave him a thread to find his way out. The bull imagery and maze-like palace of Knossos may lie behind the story."],["Akrotiri","On Santorini, the volcanic eruption around 1600 BC buried the town of Akrotiri under ash, preserving multi-story houses and beautiful wall paintings, rather like Pompeii. Unlike at Pompeii, few bodies have been found, suggesting the inhabitants escaped before the eruption."]],
sp:["Bull-leaping","One of the most famous Minoan frescoes shows young athletes grasping the horns of a charging bull and somersaulting over its back. Whether this was a sport, a religious ritual or both is unknown, but bulls clearly held great importance in Minoan culture."],
g:[["Bronze Age","The period when people made tools and weapons of bronze"],["Fresco","A painting made on wet plaster"],["Labyrinth","A complex maze"],["Decipher","To work out the meaning of a script or code"]],
f:["Linear A has never been deciphered.","The palace of Knossos had over a thousand rooms.","The Minoans had drains and flushing water systems."],
fun:"The name Minoan was invented by archaeologist Arthur Evans; we do not know what the Minoans called themselves.",
tl:"Ang mga Minoan ay nagtayo ng mayamang sibilisasyon noong Panahon ng Tanso sa pulo ng Crete sa Gresya. Nagtayo sila ng malalaking palasyo gaya ng Knossos na may makukulay na pinta, at hindi pa rin nababasa ang kanilang sulat na Linear A."});

ADDART({id:"olmecs",t:"The Olmecs",c:"History",i:"🗿",w:"Olmecs",r:["maya","aztec","mexicocountry","archaeology","writing"],
p:[["The first great civilization of Mexico","The Olmecs flourished on Mexico’s Gulf Coast about 3,000 years ago."],["Colossal heads","They carved giant stone heads, some taller than a person."],["Influencing others","Many later Mesoamerican cultures shared ideas that appear first among the Olmecs."]],
l:"The Olmecs were the earliest known major civilization of Mesoamerica, flourishing in the tropical lowlands of the Gulf Coast of Mexico, in present-day Veracruz and Tabasco, from about 1200 to 400 BC. They built the first large ceremonial centers in the region, at San Lorenzo and later La Venta, with earthen pyramids, plazas and monuments. They are best known for their colossal stone heads, carved from huge boulders of basalt, which probably portray rulers. The Olmecs traded jade, obsidian and other goods over long distances, and practices that appear early among them, such as the ballgame, monumental art, calendars and possibly writing, later became widespread in Mesoamerica, influencing the Maya and others.",
s:[["Centers of power","San Lorenzo, the first great Olmec center, flourished from about 1200 to 900 BC on a raised plateau above a river. After it declined, La Venta, on an island in a swamp, became the main center, with a large earthen pyramid and buried offerings of jade and serpentine. Thousands of people lived around these centers, farming maize, beans and squash."],["The colossal heads","Seventeen colossal heads have been found, ranging from about 1.5 to 3.4 meters tall and weighing up to around 40 tonnes. Each has a distinct face and wears a helmet-like headdress, suggesting they portray individual rulers. The basalt was brought from the Tuxtla Mountains, many kilometers away, probably by dragging and rafting."],["Art and beliefs","Olmec artists carved fine jade figures, masks and celts, as well as thrones and stelae. A common figure combines human and jaguar features, suggesting the jaguar was important in their religion. They also made pottery and figurines, and may have practiced early forms of the Mesoamerican ballgame, which used rubber balls."],["Legacy","Because many later traditions appear first among the Olmecs, they were once called the ‘mother culture’ of Mesoamerica, though scholars now see many cultures developing and influencing each other. The Cascajal Block, a stone with carved symbols from about 900 BC, may be the oldest writing in the Americas, though this is debated."]],
k:[["Location","Gulf Coast of Mexico"],["Period","About 1200–400 BC"],["Main centers","San Lorenzo, La Venta"],["Famous art","Colossal stone heads"],["Heads found","17"]],
d:[["c.1600 BC","Rubber balls are offered at El Manatí, a sacred spring"],["c.1200 BC","San Lorenzo rises as a major center"],["c.900 BC","La Venta becomes the main center"],["c.900 BC","The Cascajal Block, a possible early script"],["c.400 BC","Olmec civilization declines"],["1862","The first colossal head is found"]],
s2:[["Finding the first head","In 1862, a farm worker in Veracruz found what he thought was the bottom of a huge buried pot. It turned out to be the top of a giant stone head. It was the first colossal Olmec head discovered, though scholars did not understand its age and importance until the 20th century."],["Rubber people","The name Olmec comes from a Nahuatl word, used by the later Aztecs, meaning ‘rubber people’, after the rubber trees of the Gulf Coast. We do not know what the Olmecs called themselves. Rubber balls found at the sacred spring of El Manatí are among the oldest known in the world."]],
sp:["The were-jaguar","Many Olmec carvings show a figure with a baby-like face, a snarling mouth and jaguar features, called a ‘were-jaguar’ by archaeologists. It may represent a god or a spirit connected with rain, rulers or the power of transformation between human and animal."],
g:[["Mesoamerica","The region of ancient civilizations in Mexico and Central America"],["Basalt","A hard, dark volcanic rock"],["Jade","A hard green stone prized for carving"],["Stela","A carved upright stone slab"]],
f:["The largest Olmec heads weigh around 40 tonnes.","Seventeen colossal heads have been found.","The name Olmec means ‘rubber people’."],
fun:"Each Olmec colossal head has a different face, as if they were portraits of real people.",
tl:"Ang mga Olmec ang unang malaking sibilisasyon sa Mesoamerica, sa baybayin ng Golpo ng Mexico mga 3,000 taon na ang nakararaan. Kilala sila sa malalaking ulong bato, at maraming sumunod na kultura ang nagmana ng kanilang mga ideya."});

ADDART({id:"songhai",t:"The Songhai Empire",c:"History",i:"🏜️",w:"Songhai Empire",r:["mali","mansamusa","islam","sahel","africacolon"],
p:[["A West African giant","The Songhai Empire was one of the largest empires in African history."],["Along the Niger","It controlled trade along the Niger River and across the Sahara."],["Cities of learning","Timbuktu and Gao were centers of trade and Islamic scholarship."]],
l:"The Songhai Empire was a powerful state in West Africa during the 15th and 16th centuries, one of the largest empires in African history. Centered on the bend of the Niger River, with its capital at Gao in present-day Mali, it grew from a small kingdom into an empire that replaced the Mali Empire as the dominant power of the western Sahel. Under Sunni Ali and Askia Muhammad, it controlled trade routes across the Sahara that carried gold, salt, cloth and enslaved people, and great cities such as Timbuktu and Djenné. Timbuktu became famous for its scholars, mosques and manuscripts. The empire fell after a Moroccan army armed with guns defeated it at the Battle of Tondibi in 1591.",
s:[["Rise","The Songhai people lived along the middle Niger, fishing, farming and trading, and were for a time ruled by the Mali Empire. As Mali weakened in the 15th century, the Songhai ruler Sunni Ali, who reigned from 1464 to 1492, built a strong army and a fleet of river boats and conquered Timbuktu in 1468 and Djenné in 1473."],["Askia the Great","In 1493, a general named Muhammad Touré seized power and founded the Askia dynasty. Askia Muhammad, known as Askia the Great, organized the empire into provinces with governors, standardized weights and measures and supported Islamic learning. In 1496–1497, he made a pilgrimage to Mecca, where he was named caliph of West Africa."],["Timbuktu","Under Songhai rule, Timbuktu was a thriving city of trade and scholarship. Scholars taught law, astronomy, mathematics, medicine and theology at mosques such as Sankore, and wealthy families collected manuscripts. Traders exchanged gold from the south for salt from Saharan mines such as Taghaza."],["Fall","In 1591, the sultan of Morocco sent an army across the Sahara to seize Songhai’s gold. Although much smaller, the Moroccan force had guns and cannons and defeated the Songhai army at the Battle of Tondibi. The empire broke apart into smaller states, and trade shifted towards the Atlantic coast."]],
k:[["Capital","Gao"],["Height","15th–16th centuries"],["Great rulers","Sunni Ali, Askia Muhammad"],["Famous city","Timbuktu"],["Fell","1591, Battle of Tondibi"]],
d:[["1464","Sunni Ali becomes ruler"],["1468","Songhai conquers Timbuktu"],["1473","Songhai conquers Djenné"],["1493","Askia Muhammad takes power"],["1496–97","Askia Muhammad’s pilgrimage to Mecca"],["1591","Defeat at the Battle of Tondibi"]],
s2:[["Salt for gold","In West Africa, salt was so valuable that it was traded weight for weight with gold in some places. Camel caravans carried slabs of salt from mines in the Sahara south to Timbuktu, where it was exchanged for gold, ivory and kola nuts from the forest regions."],["The Tomb of Askia","In Gao stands the Tomb of Askia, a 17-meter pyramid-shaped tower of mud brick built in the late 15th century, believed to be the burial place of Askia Muhammad. With its wooden beams sticking out of the walls, it is a fine example of Sahelian mud-brick architecture and a UNESCO World Heritage Site."]],
sp:["River power","The Songhai used the Niger River as a highway. Their war canoes and boats carried soldiers and goods, and control of the river allowed them to tax trade and move armies quickly across their vast lands."],
g:[["Sahel","The semi-arid region just south of the Sahara"],["Caravan","A group of traders traveling together, often with camels"],["Askia","The title of the rulers of the second Songhai dynasty"],["Manuscript","A book or document written by hand"]],
f:["Songhai was one of the largest empires in African history.","Its capital was Gao, on the Niger River.","Salt was traded for gold in its markets."],
fun:"Timbuktu became so famous in Europe that its name is still used to mean a faraway, almost mythical place.",
tl:"Ang Imperyong Songhai ay isa sa pinakamalalaking imperyo sa kasaysayan ng Africa, sa kahabaan ng Ilog Niger. Kinontrol nito ang kalakalan sa Sahara, at naging sentro ng kalakalan at karunungang Islamiko ang Timbuktu at Gao."});

ADDART({id:"zulu",t:"The Zulu Kingdom",c:"History",i:"🛡️",w:"Zulu Kingdom",r:["southafricacountry","africacolon","apartheid","mandela","martial"],
p:[["A southern African power","The Zulu Kingdom rose to power in southeastern Africa in the early 1800s."],["Shaka’s army","King Shaka built a powerful army with new weapons and tactics."],["War with Britain","In 1879, Zulu forces defeated a British army at Isandlwana before the kingdom was conquered."]],
l:"The Zulu Kingdom was a powerful state in southeastern Africa, in what is now the province of KwaZulu-Natal in South Africa. It was created in the early 19th century by Shaka kaSenzangakhona, who united many clans and built a disciplined army that dominated the region. The kingdom later clashed with Boer settlers and the British Empire. In 1879, during the Anglo-Zulu War, Zulu warriors inflicted a famous defeat on British forces at Isandlwana, but the British went on to win the war and broke up the kingdom. Today, the Zulu people are the largest ethnic group in South Africa, isiZulu is the most widely spoken home language there, and the Zulu king remains an important traditional leader.",
s:[["Shaka","Shaka became leader of the small Zulu clan around 1816. He reorganized the army into regiments based on age, trained them hard and introduced a short stabbing spear and large shields for close combat, as well as the ‘buffalo horns’ formation, in which side forces surrounded the enemy. Through conquest and alliances, the Zulu Kingdom grew rapidly. Shaka was assassinated in 1828 by his half-brothers."],["Upheaval in southern Africa","The rise of the Zulu and other states in the early 19th century was part of a period of wars and migrations across southern Africa, sometimes called the Mfecane. Historians debate its causes, which may include drought, competition for land and trade, and pressure from European colonists."],["Boers and British","In 1838, Boer settlers known as Voortrekkers defeated a Zulu army at the Battle of Blood River. Later, Britain sought to bring the region under its control. In 1879, the British invaded. On 22 January, Zulu forces overwhelmed a British camp at Isandlwana, one of the worst defeats of a colonial army in Africa. But in July, the British captured the royal kraal at Ulundi, and King Cetshwayo was captured."],["The Zulu today","After the war, Britain divided the kingdom, and it was later absorbed into the colony of Natal and then South Africa. The Zulu people kept their language, culture and monarchy. Today about 12 million people speak isiZulu as their home language. Celebrations such as the annual Reed Dance and traditional beadwork, music and dance remain important."]],
k:[["Location","KwaZulu-Natal, South Africa"],["Founder","Shaka kaSenzangakhona"],["Rose to power","About 1816–1828"],["Famous battle","Isandlwana, 1879"],["Language","isiZulu"]],
d:[["c.1816","Shaka becomes leader of the Zulu"],["1828","Shaka is assassinated"],["1838","Battle of Blood River"],["22 Jan 1879","Zulu victory at Isandlwana"],["Jul 1879","British victory at Ulundi"],["1897","Zululand is absorbed into Natal"]],
s2:[["Isandlwana","At Isandlwana, around 20,000 Zulu warriors attacked a British camp of about 1,700 soldiers and African allies beneath a rocky hill. Using the buffalo horns formation, they surrounded and overran the camp. More than 1,300 of the British force were killed. The defeat shocked Britain."],["Beadwork messages","Zulu beadwork is famous for its bright colors and patterns. Traditionally, combinations of colors and shapes could carry messages, for example about love or marital status, so a beaded necklace could act like a letter. Today beadwork is worn at ceremonies and sold as art."]],
sp:["The Reed Dance","Every September, tens of thousands of young Zulu women take part in the Umkhosi woMhlanga, or Reed Dance, at the royal palace, carrying tall reeds to present to the king. The ceremony celebrates Zulu culture and womanhood and is one of the largest cultural gatherings in southern Africa."],
g:[["Regiment","A large unit of an army"],["Kraal","A traditional enclosure for homes or cattle"],["Voortrekkers","Dutch-speaking settlers who moved inland from the Cape in the 1830s"],["isiZulu","The language of the Zulu people"]],
f:["isiZulu is the most widely spoken home language in South Africa.","Zulu forces won the Battle of Isandlwana in 1879.","Shaka ruled the Zulu for about 12 years."],
fun:"The word Zulu means ‘heaven’ or ‘sky’ in isiZulu, and amaZulu means ‘people of the heavens’.",
tl:"Ang Kahariang Zulu ay naging makapangyarihan sa timog-silangang Africa noong unang bahagi ng 1800. Nagtayo si Haring Shaka ng malakas na hukbo, at noong 1879 ay tinalo ng mga Zulu ang isang hukbong British sa Isandlwana bago sila nasakop."});

ADDART({id:"inuit",t:"The Inuit",c:"Society",i:"🛶",w:"Inuit",r:["arctic","tundra","greenland","canadacountry","indigrelig"],
p:[["People of the Arctic","The Inuit are Indigenous peoples of the Arctic in Greenland, Canada and Alaska."],["Clever inventions","They invented the kayak, the snow house and warm, waterproof clothing."],["Self-government","Inuit lead their own governments in Nunavut, Greenland and other regions."]],
l:"The Inuit are Indigenous peoples of the Arctic, living in Greenland, northern Canada, Alaska and a small part of the Russian Far East. There are around 180,000 Inuit. Their ancestors, the Thule people, spread east from Alaska across the Arctic around 1,000 years ago. Living in one of the harshest environments on Earth, the Inuit developed remarkable technologies and knowledge: kayaks and larger umiak boats, dog sleds, snow houses, harpoons, and warm, waterproof clothing made from animal skins. Traditionally, they hunted seals, whales, walruses, caribou and fish. Today, most Inuit live in modern towns, but hunting, the Inuit languages and traditional knowledge remain central to their culture. Many consider the older term ‘Eskimo’ offensive.",
s:[["Living with the Arctic","The Inuit moved with the seasons to follow animals, hunting seals at breathing holes in the sea ice in winter and caribou and fish in summer. Every part of an animal was used: meat for food, fat for fuel and light in stone lamps, skins for clothing and tents, bones and ivory for tools. Sharing food was essential to survival."],["Inventions","The kayak, a light, narrow boat covered with skins, allowed hunters to travel silently. Snow houses, often called igloos, were built from blocks of packed snow as winter homes or temporary shelters. The parka and anorak are Inuit inventions, as are snow goggles with narrow slits to prevent snow blindness."],["Contact and change","Contact with European whalers, traders and missionaries from the 18th and 19th centuries brought trade goods but also diseases. In the 20th century, governments moved many Inuit into permanent settlements and sent children to residential schools, where their language and culture were suppressed. Inuit have since campaigned for their rights and land."],["Self-government today","In 1999, Canada created the territory of Nunavut, meaning ‘our land’, where most people are Inuit and Inuktitut is an official language. Greenland, whose population is mostly Inuit, has had self-government within the Kingdom of Denmark since 1979, expanded in 2009. Inuit are also leading voices on climate change, which is melting the sea ice they depend on."]],
k:[["Where","Greenland, Canada, Alaska, Russian Far East"],["Population","About 180,000"],["Ancestors","The Thule people"],["Main language","Inuktitut and related languages"],["Canadian territory","Nunavut, since 1999"]],
d:[["c.1000","Thule ancestors spread east from Alaska"],["1700s–1800s","Contact with European whalers and traders"],["1950s","Many Inuit moved into permanent settlements"],["1979","Greenland gains home rule"],["1999","Nunavut is created in Canada"],["2009","Greenland gains expanded self-government"]],
s2:[["The inuksuk","An inuksuk is a stone figure built by stacking rocks, sometimes in a human-like shape. Inuksuit served as landmarks for travelers, markers of good hunting or fishing places, or memorials. The word means ‘in the likeness of a human’. An inuksuk appeared on the logo of the 2010 Vancouver Winter Olympics."],["Throat singing","Inuit throat singing, called katajjaq in Canada, is traditionally performed by two women facing each other, making rhythmic sounds from deep in the throat in a kind of game until one laughs or runs out of breath. Once discouraged by missionaries, it has been revived and is performed around the world."]],
sp:["Snow houses","An igloo built from blocks of firm snow can be surprisingly warm. Snow is a good insulator, and a small entrance tunnel set lower than the living area traps warm air inside. With body heat and a seal-oil lamp, the inside could be many degrees warmer than the freezing air outside."],
g:[["Inuk","One Inuit person"],["Kayak","A small, narrow boat moved with a double-bladed paddle"],["Inuksuk","A stone landmark built by the Inuit"],["Nunavut","A Canadian territory governed mainly by Inuit"]],
f:["The parka and the kayak are Inuit inventions.","Nunavut means ‘our land’ in Inuktitut.","One person is an Inuk; Inuit means ‘the people’."],
fun:"Inuit snow goggles, carved from bone or wood with narrow slits, protected eyes from snow glare long before sunglasses existed.",
tl:"Ang mga Inuit ay mga katutubo ng Arctic sa Greenland, Canada at Alaska. Sila ang nag-imbento ng kayak, ng bahay na niyebe at ng mainit na damit, at pinamumunuan nila ang sarili nilang pamahalaan sa Nunavut at Greenland."});

ADDART({id:"maori",t:"The Māori",c:"Society",i:"🛶",w:"Māori people",r:["nzcountry","polynesia","indigrelig","ships","olymp"],
p:[["New Zealand’s first people","The Māori are the Indigenous Polynesian people of New Zealand, or Aotearoa."],["Great voyagers","Their ancestors sailed across the Pacific in canoes around 700 years ago."],["A living culture","Te reo Māori, the haka and tā moko tattoos are part of New Zealand life today."]],
l:"The Māori are the Indigenous Polynesian people of New Zealand, known in Māori as Aotearoa. Their ancestors arrived from eastern Polynesia in a series of canoe voyages around 1250–1300, making New Zealand one of the last major landmasses on Earth to be settled by humans. Over the following centuries, they developed a distinct culture organized around tribes called iwi, with rich traditions of carving, weaving, tattooing, oratory and the performance known as haka. British colonization in the 19th century, the Treaty of Waitangi of 1840 and the wars and land losses that followed brought great hardship. Today, about one in six New Zealanders is Māori, and Māori language and culture are an important part of national identity.",
s:[["Voyaging to Aotearoa","Polynesian navigators crossed thousands of kilometers of open ocean in large double-hulled canoes, called waka, navigating by the stars, waves, winds and birds. Each iwi traces its ancestry to particular canoes. The settlers adapted to New Zealand’s cooler climate, growing sweet potatoes, called kūmara, and hunting large flightless birds called moa, which soon became extinct."],["Society and culture","Māori society was organized into iwi, sub-tribes called hapū and extended families, called whānau. The marae, a meeting ground with a carved meeting house, is the heart of community life, where guests are welcomed, debates held and the dead mourned. Māori carvers decorated meeting houses and canoes, and tā moko, traditional tattooing, recorded a person’s identity and ancestry."],["The Treaty of Waitangi","On 6 February 1840, the British Crown and many Māori chiefs signed the Treaty of Waitangi, Te Tiriti o Waitangi. The English and Māori versions differ in important ways, especially about sovereignty. Conflicts over land led to the New Zealand Wars of the 1840s to 1870s, and much Māori land was confiscated or sold."],["Māori today","In the 20th century, Māori moved into cities, and the language declined. From the 1970s, a revival brought Māori-language preschools, called kōhanga reo, and in 1987 te reo Māori became an official language. The Waitangi Tribunal investigates breaches of the treaty, and settlements have returned some land and money to iwi. Matariki, the Māori New Year, became a public holiday in 2022."]],
k:[["Country","New Zealand (Aotearoa)"],["Arrived","About 1250–1300"],["Population","About 1 in 6 New Zealanders"],["Language","Te reo Māori (official since 1987)"],["Treaty","Treaty of Waitangi, 1840"]],
d:[["c.1250–1300","Polynesian voyagers settle Aotearoa"],["1642","Dutch explorer Abel Tasman arrives"],["1840","The Treaty of Waitangi is signed"],["1845–1872","The New Zealand Wars"],["1987","Te reo Māori becomes an official language"],["2022","Matariki becomes a public holiday"]],
s2:[["The haka","The haka is a ceremonial dance with chanting, stamping, facial expressions and strong movements, performed to welcome guests, honor achievements or prepare for battle. New Zealand’s rugby team, the All Blacks, perform the haka ‘Ka Mate’ before matches, making it famous worldwide."],["Matariki","Matariki is the Māori name for the Pleiades star cluster, whose rise in midwinter marks the Māori New Year. It is a time to remember those who have died, celebrate the present and plan for the future. Since 2022, it has been a public holiday in New Zealand."]],
sp:["Waka navigation","Modern voyagers have revived traditional Polynesian navigation, sailing double-hulled canoes across the Pacific without instruments, reading the stars, swells and birds. These voyages have proved how the ancestors of the Māori could find tiny islands in the vast ocean."],
g:[["Iwi","A Māori tribe"],["Marae","A Māori meeting ground and community center"],["Waka","A Māori canoe"],["Te reo Māori","The Māori language"]],
f:["The Māori arrived in New Zealand around 1250–1300.","Te reo Māori has been an official language since 1987.","Waitangi Day, 6 February, is New Zealand’s national day."],
fun:"Many New Zealanders greet each other with ‘kia ora’, a Māori phrase meaning ‘be well’ or ‘hello’.",
tl:"Ang mga Māori ang katutubong Polynesian ng New Zealand o Aotearoa. Naglayag ang kanilang mga ninuno sa Pasipiko sakay ng mga bangka mga 700 taon na ang nakararaan, at bahagi ng buhay sa New Zealand ngayon ang kanilang wika, haka at tā moko."});

ADDART({id:"aboriginal",t:"Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Peoples",c:"Society",i:"🪃",w:"Aboriginal Australians",r:["australiacountry","caveart","indigrelig","hrights","constellations"],
p:[["Australia’s First Peoples","Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have lived in Australia for at least 50,000 years."],["Oldest living cultures","They belong to the oldest continuous cultures on Earth."],["Many nations","Before colonization, there were over 250 language groups across the continent."]],
l:"Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples are the First Peoples of Australia. Aboriginal people have lived on the continent for at least 50,000 years, and possibly 65,000 years, making their cultures among the oldest continuous cultures in the world. Torres Strait Islanders, from the islands between Australia and Papua New Guinea, are a distinct Melanesian people. Before British colonization began in 1788, hundreds of nations lived across Australia, speaking more than 250 languages, each with deep connections to their Country, the land, waters and skies they care for. Colonization brought violence, disease, dispossession and the forced removal of children. Today, around 4 percent of Australians identify as Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander.",
s:[["Connection to Country","For Aboriginal peoples, land is not simply property but a living relationship, carrying responsibilities to care for it. Knowledge about the land, plants, animals, seasons and stars was passed down through stories, songs, dances and art. Many of these are linked to the Dreaming, which describes how ancestral beings shaped the world and established law and ways of living."],["Art and knowledge","Aboriginal rock art, some of it tens of thousands of years old, is found across Australia, especially in the Kimberley and Arnhem Land. People managed the land with ‘cultural burning’, lighting small fires to encourage new growth and reduce the risk of large bushfires. They used tools such as boomerangs, spear-throwers and fish traps, some of which are thousands of years old."],["Colonization","From 1788, British settlement spread across the continent, often through violence, and the land was treated as if it had belonged to no one, under the idea of terra nullius. Many people died from disease and conflict. For much of the 20th century, governments removed many Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children from their families; they are known as the Stolen Generations."],["Rights and recognition","In a 1967 referendum, Australians voted to count Aboriginal people in the census and allow national laws for them. In 1992, the High Court’s Mabo decision rejected terra nullius and recognized native title to land. In 2008, the prime minister formally apologized to the Stolen Generations. In 2023, a proposal to create an Indigenous ‘Voice to Parliament’ was rejected in a referendum."]],
k:[["Where","Australia and the Torres Strait Islands"],["Time in Australia","At least 50,000 years"],["Languages at colonization","More than 250"],["Share of Australians","About 4%"],["Land rights case","Mabo decision, 1992"]],
d:[["50,000+ years ago","Aboriginal people live in Australia"],["1788","British colonization begins"],["1967","A referendum changes the constitution"],["1992","The Mabo decision recognizes native title"],["2008","National Apology to the Stolen Generations"],["2023","The Voice to Parliament referendum is defeated"]],
s2:[["Uluru","Uluru, a giant red sandstone rock in central Australia, is sacred to the Anangu people, its traditional owners. It was returned to Anangu ownership in 1985 and is jointly managed with the national park service. Since 2019, climbing Uluru has been banned out of respect for its cultural significance."],["Astronomy","Aboriginal peoples observed the night sky closely, using stars to mark seasons, guide travel and tell stories. One famous figure is the Emu in the Sky, formed by dark dust clouds in the Milky Way, whose changing position through the year signaled when emus were laying eggs."]],
sp:["The flags","The Aboriginal flag, designed by Luritja artist Harold Thomas in 1971, is black for the people, red for the earth and a yellow circle for the sun. The Torres Strait Islander flag, designed in 1992, features a white dhari, a traditional headdress, with a star. Both are official flags of Australia."],
g:[["Country","The land, waters and skies to which a people are connected"],["Dreaming","Beliefs about creation, law and ancestral beings"],["Native title","Recognition of Indigenous rights to land"],["Stolen Generations","Children forcibly removed from their families"]],
f:["Aboriginal cultures are among the oldest continuous cultures on Earth.","More than 250 languages were spoken in Australia in 1788.","Climbing Uluru has been banned since 2019."],
fun:"The boomerang that returns to the thrower was mainly used for sport and play; hunting boomerangs were usually designed not to return.",
tl:"Ang mga Aboriginal at Torres Strait Islander ang mga unang tao ng Australia, na naninirahan doon nang hindi bababa sa 50,000 taon. Kabilang sila sa pinakamatatandang tuloy-tuloy na kultura sa mundo, at mahigit 250 wika ang sinasalita bago ang kolonisasyon."});

ADDART({id:"sumo",t:"Sumo",c:"How We Live",i:"🤼",w:"Sumo",r:["japancountry","shinto","martial","japan","olymp"],
p:[["Japan’s national sport","Sumo is a form of wrestling in which two wrestlers try to push each other out of a ring."],["Ancient rituals","Matches include Shinto rituals such as throwing salt to purify the ring."],["Living the life","Professional wrestlers live and train together in stables under strict rules."]],
l:"Sumo is a Japanese form of wrestling in which two wrestlers, called rikishi, face each other in a circular ring and try to force their opponent out of the ring or make any part of his body other than the soles of his feet touch the ground. Bouts often last only a few seconds. Sumo has a history of over 1,500 years and is closely linked to Shinto religion, with many rituals, such as throwing salt into the ring to purify it. Professional sumo is regarded as Japan’s national sport. Wrestlers wear a silk belt called a mawashi and their hair in a traditional topknot, and live in training stables. There are no weight classes, so smaller wrestlers can face giants.",
s:[["The ring and the rules","The ring, called the dohyō, is a raised platform of clay with a circle 4.55 meters across, marked by straw bales. Wrestlers win by pushing, throwing, tripping or slapping their opponent out of the ring or to the ground. Punching, kicking, hair-pulling and choking are forbidden. There are dozens of recognized winning techniques."],["Rituals","Before a bout, wrestlers stamp their feet to drive away evil spirits, clap their hands, and throw handfuls of salt into the ring to purify it. A roof shaped like a Shinto shrine hangs above the ring. The referee, called the gyōji, wears colorful robes inspired by ancient court dress."],["Life in a stable","Professional sumo wrestlers live in stables, called heya, under a stablemaster. Junior wrestlers do chores, cook and serve seniors. Wrestlers train early in the morning and eat a hearty stew called chankonabe. Many top wrestlers weigh 150 kilograms or more, but they are also flexible, quick and strong."],["Tournaments and ranks","There are six grand tournaments a year, each lasting 15 days, three in Tokyo and one each in Osaka, Nagoya and Fukuoka. Wrestlers rise and fall through the ranks based on results. The highest rank, yokozuna, is held by very few and is never lost through defeat; a yokozuna who performs poorly is expected to retire."]],
k:[["Country","Japan"],["Ring","Dohyō, 4.55 m across"],["Tournaments","Six a year, 15 days each"],["Highest rank","Yokozuna"],["Weight classes","None"]],
d:[["c.AD 700s","Sumo is performed at the imperial court"],["1600s","Professional sumo develops in the Edo period"],["1909","The Kokugikan arena opens in Tokyo"],["1993","Akebono becomes the first foreign-born yokozuna"],["2021","Hakuhō retires with a record 45 top-division titles"]],
s2:[["Foreign champions","Since the 1990s, wrestlers from outside Japan have risen to the top of sumo. Akebono, from Hawaii, became the first foreign-born yokozuna in 1993. Mongolian wrestlers have been especially successful, including Hakuhō, who won a record 45 top-division championships before retiring in 2021."],["Amateur and women’s sumo","Professional sumo is only for men, and women are traditionally not allowed in the professional ring. However, amateur sumo is practiced around the world by both men and women, with world championships held since the 1990s and separate weight categories."]],
sp:["The topknot","Sumo wrestlers grow their hair long and wear it in a topknot called a chonmage, a hairstyle of the samurai era. The topknot is cut off in a special ceremony when a wrestler retires, often with hundreds of guests each taking a symbolic snip."],
g:[["Rikishi","A sumo wrestler"],["Dohyō","The sumo ring"],["Mawashi","The belt worn by sumo wrestlers"],["Yokozuna","The highest rank in sumo"]],
f:["Sumo bouts often last only a few seconds.","The sumo ring is 4.55 meters across.","Sumo has no weight classes."],
fun:"Sumo wrestlers throw salt into the ring before each bout; top wrestlers can throw several hundred grams at a time.",
tl:"Ang sumo ay uri ng pakikipagbuno sa Hapon kung saan sinisikap ng dalawang mambubuno na itulak ang isa’t isa palabas ng bilog. May mga ritwal itong Shinto gaya ng paghahagis ng asin, at ito ang pambansang laro ng Hapon."});

ADDART({id:"gymnastics",t:"Gymnastics",c:"How We Live",i:"🤸",w:"Gymnastics",r:["olymp","athletics","exercise","greece","bones"],
p:[["Strength and grace","Gymnastics combines strength, flexibility, balance and control."],["Many events","Gymnasts compete on apparatus such as the beam, rings, bars and floor."],["An Olympic favorite","It has been part of the modern Olympic Games since 1896."]],
l:"Gymnastics is a sport in which athletes perform routines of jumps, flips, twists, swings, balances and handstands that require strength, flexibility, agility, coordination and courage. Its name comes from the ancient Greek word gymnos, meaning ‘naked’, because Greek athletes trained without clothes. Modern gymnastics developed in Europe in the early 19th century and has been part of every modern Olympic Games since 1896. The main forms are artistic gymnastics, performed on apparatus such as the vault, bars, beam and rings; rhythmic gymnastics, using ribbons, hoops, balls, clubs and ropes; and trampoline. Gymnasts are judged on the difficulty and execution of their routines.",
s:[["Origins","In ancient Greece, physical training in the gymnasium was part of education. Modern gymnastics began in Germany, where Friedrich Ludwig Jahn opened an outdoor gymnasium in 1811 and introduced apparatus such as the parallel bars, rings and horizontal bar. In Sweden, Per Henrik Ling developed a system focused on health and movement."],["Artistic gymnastics","In men’s artistic gymnastics there are six events: floor, pommel horse, still rings, vault, parallel bars and high bar. Women compete in four: vault, uneven bars, balance beam and floor. Gymnasts also compete in the all-around, combining all events, and in team competitions."],["Rhythmic and trampoline","Rhythmic gymnastics, performed by women at the Olympics, combines ballet, dance and gymnastics with hand apparatus such as ribbons and hoops, set to music. It became an Olympic sport in 1984. Trampoline gymnastics, with athletes bouncing up to about 10 meters high, joined the Olympics in 2000."],["Judging","Since 2006, scores have combined a difficulty score, which rises with harder skills, and an execution score for how well they are performed, starting from 10 and reduced for mistakes. Before then, a perfect score was 10.0, first awarded at the Olympics to Nadia Comăneci in 1976."]],
k:[["Word origin","Greek gymnos, ‘naked’"],["Olympic since","1896"],["Men’s artistic events","Six"],["Women’s artistic events","Four"],["First Olympic perfect 10","Nadia Comăneci, 1976"]],
d:[["1811","Friedrich Ludwig Jahn opens an outdoor gymnasium in Berlin"],["1881","The international gymnastics federation is founded"],["1896","Gymnastics at the first modern Olympics"],["1928","Women’s gymnastics first held at the Olympics"],["1976","Nadia Comăneci scores the first perfect 10"],["2000","Trampoline joins the Olympics"]],
s2:[["The perfect 10","At the 1976 Montreal Olympics, 14-year-old Nadia Comăneci of Romania scored a perfect 10.0 on the uneven bars. The scoreboard had only three digits, so it displayed ‘1.00’. She went on to score six more perfect 10s at those Games."],["Simone Biles","American gymnast Simone Biles is the most decorated gymnast in history, with more than 40 Olympic and World Championship medals. Several skills are named after her because she was the first to perform them in competition. In 2021, she withdrew from some Olympic events to protect her mental health, sparking a worldwide conversation."]],
sp:["Skills named after gymnasts","When a gymnast is the first to perform a new skill successfully at a major competition, it can be named after them. There are skills named after gymnasts from countries around the world, from the Yurchenko vault, named after Soviet gymnast Natalia Yurchenko, to several ‘Biles’ elements."],
g:[["Apparatus","The equipment used in gymnastics, such as the beam or rings"],["Routine","A planned sequence of skills"],["All-around","A competition combining all events"],["Vault","An event where gymnasts spring off a table into flips and twists"]],
f:["The word gymnastics comes from a Greek word meaning ‘naked’.","The balance beam is only 10 centimeters wide.","Nadia Comăneci scored the first Olympic perfect 10 in 1976."],
fun:"When Nadia Comăneci scored her perfect 10, the scoreboard showed 1.00 because it could not display a 10.",
tl:"Ang himnastiko ay larong pinagsasama ang lakas, pagkabaluktot, balanse at kontrol. Gumagamit ang mga gymnast ng beam, rings, bars at floor, at bahagi ito ng modernong Olympics mula pa noong 1896."});

ADDART({id:"steel",t:"Iron and Steel",c:"Technology",i:"⚙️",w:"Steel",r:["metals","indrev","bridges","skyscrapers","concrete"],
p:[["The metal of the modern world","Steel, made mostly from iron, is the world’s most important building metal."],["From the Iron Age","People have smelted iron for over 3,000 years."],["Cleaner steel","Steelmaking produces a lot of carbon dioxide, so new low-carbon methods are being developed."]],
l:"Iron is one of the most common elements on Earth and the main ingredient of steel, an alloy of iron with a small amount of carbon, usually less than 2 percent, and often other elements. Steel is strong, tough and relatively cheap, and it is used in buildings, bridges, ships, cars, railways, tools, machines and appliances; about 1.9 billion tonnes are produced each year. People began smelting iron from ore over 3,000 years ago, starting the Iron Age. Mass production of steel began with the Bessemer process in the 1850s, helping to power the Industrial Revolution. Steel is also the world’s most recycled material. Because steelmaking produces around 7 percent of global carbon dioxide emissions, companies are developing cleaner methods using hydrogen and electricity.",
s:[["The Iron Age","Early people used iron from meteorites, which fell from space. Iron smelting, heating iron ore with charcoal in a furnace to extract the metal, spread across the Middle East and beyond from about 1200 BC. Iron tools and weapons were harder and cheaper than bronze, changing farming and warfare. In China, iron was being cast in molds by about the 5th century BC."],["From iron to steel","Pure iron is soft; adding a little carbon makes steel, which is much harder and stronger. For centuries steel was difficult to make and expensive, used mainly for blades. In 1856, Henry Bessemer invented a process that blew air through molten iron to remove impurities, making steel cheaply in large amounts. Railways, ships, bridges and skyscrapers soon followed."],["How steel is made","Most steel is made by melting iron ore with coke, made from coal, and limestone in a blast furnace to produce iron, then blowing oxygen through the molten iron to make steel. About a third of steel is made in electric arc furnaces, which melt scrap steel using electricity. The molten steel is then shaped into slabs, bars, beams and sheets."],["Types of steel","Adding different elements gives steel special properties. Stainless steel, invented in 1913 by Harry Brearley, contains chromium, which stops it from rusting, and is used in kitchens and hospitals. Other steels are made extra hard for tools or extra strong and light for cars."]],
k:[["Made of","Iron with a little carbon"],["Yearly production","About 1.9 billion tonnes"],["Largest producer","China, over half"],["Mass production began","1856, Bessemer process"],["Share of CO₂ emissions","About 7%"]],
d:[["c.1200 BC","The Iron Age begins in the Middle East"],["c.500 BC","Cast iron is made in China"],["1709","Abraham Darby smelts iron using coke"],["1856","Bessemer invents cheap steelmaking"],["1913","Stainless steel is invented"],["2021","First steel made with fossil-free hydrogen delivered in Sweden"]],
s2:[["Tutankhamun’s dagger","One of the daggers found in Tutankhamun’s tomb, from about 1325 BC, has a blade of iron. Scientists have shown that the iron came from a meteorite, from its high nickel content. At the time, iron was rarer and more precious than gold in Egypt."],["The Eiffel Tower","The Eiffel Tower, completed in 1889, was built from about 7,300 tonnes of wrought iron, held together by 2.5 million rivets. It was the tallest structure in the world for 41 years. Like many iron and steel structures, it must be repainted regularly to stop it rusting."]],
sp:["Recycling steel","Steel is magnetic, so it is easy to separate from other waste, and it can be melted and reused again and again without losing quality. Old cars, cans, appliances and buildings are recycled into new steel, saving energy and raw materials."],
g:[["Ore","Rock from which a metal can be extracted"],["Smelting","Heating ore to extract metal"],["Alloy","A mixture of a metal with other elements"],["Blast furnace","A tall furnace for making iron from ore"]],
f:["Steel is the most recycled material in the world.","China produces more than half of the world’s steel.","Stainless steel was invented in 1913."],
fun:"Your blood is red because of iron in hemoglobin, the molecule that carries oxygen.",
tl:"Ang bakal at asero ang pinakamahalagang metal sa pagtatayo sa makabagong mundo. Mahigit 3,000 taon nang tinutunaw ng tao ang bakal, at gumagawa ngayon ng mas malinis na paraan ng paggawa ng asero dahil malaki ang ambag nito sa carbon dioxide."});

ADDART({id:"glass",t:"Glass",c:"Technology",i:"🔍",w:"Glass",r:["stainedglass","fiberoptics","scope","venice","recycling"],
p:[["Melted sand","Glass is made mainly by melting sand at very high temperatures."],["A clear invention","People have made glass for about 4,500 years."],["Everywhere today","Windows, bottles, screens, lenses and fiber optic cables all use glass."]],
l:"Glass is a hard, usually transparent material made by melting sand, which is mostly silica, together with other ingredients such as soda and lime, at temperatures of around 1,500°C, then cooling it. Unlike most solids, glass has no regular crystal structure; its atoms are arranged randomly, like a frozen liquid, which is why it is called an amorphous solid. Natural glass forms when volcanic lava cools quickly, as obsidian, or when lightning strikes sand. People first made glass beads in Mesopotamia around 4,500 years ago. Glassblowing, invented around 2,000 years ago, made glass vessels common. Today glass is used in windows, bottles, phone screens, lenses, solar panels and the fiber optic cables that carry the internet.",
s:[["Early glass","The earliest glass objects, beads made in Mesopotamia around 2500 BC, were probably by-products of making pottery glazes or metal. Egyptians made colorful glass vessels by shaping molten glass around a core. Around the 1st century BC, craftspeople on the eastern Mediterranean coast discovered that molten glass could be inflated by blowing through a tube, and the Romans spread glassblowing across their empire."],["Venetian glass","In 1291, Venice moved its glassmakers to the island of Murano, partly to protect the city from fires and partly to keep their techniques secret. Murano glassmakers produced the clearest glass of the time, called cristallo, as well as mirrors and colorful decorative glass. Murano is still famous for its glass."],["Lenses and windows","Polished glass lenses made eyeglasses possible in Italy around 1290, and later the microscope and telescope, which transformed science. Window glass was once expensive and made in small panes. In 1959, Alastair Pilkington introduced the float glass process, in which molten glass floats on a bath of molten tin to form perfectly flat sheets, now used for almost all windows."],["High-tech glass","Special glasses can resist heat, like cookware, or be extremely strong and scratch-resistant, like phone screens. Fiber optic cables, made of ultra-pure glass strands thinner than a hair, carry internet data as pulses of light across continents and under oceans. Glass can be recycled endlessly without losing quality."]],
k:[["Main ingredient","Sand (silica)"],["Melting temperature","About 1,500°C"],["First made","About 2500 BC, Mesopotamia"],["Glassblowing invented","About 1st century BC"],["Flat glass process","Float glass, 1959"]],
d:[["c.2500 BC","Glass beads in Mesopotamia"],["c.1500 BC","Egyptian glass vessels"],["c.50 BC","Glassblowing is invented"],["1291","Venice moves glassmakers to Murano"],["c.1290","Eyeglasses are invented in Italy"],["1959","The float glass process"]],
s2:[["Obsidian","Obsidian is a natural black volcanic glass that forms when lava cools too quickly for crystals to grow. Because it breaks into very sharp edges, people around the world made knives, arrowheads and mirrors from it for thousands of years. The Aztecs used obsidian blades in their weapons."],["Is glass a liquid?","A popular myth says old church windows are thicker at the bottom because glass slowly flows over centuries. In fact, glass is a solid at room temperature. Old windows are uneven because of the way glass was made then, and glaziers often placed the thicker edge at the bottom."]],
sp:["Gorilla Glass","The tough glass on many smartphone screens is made by dipping glass in a bath of hot molten salt, which swaps small sodium atoms in its surface for larger potassium atoms. This squeezes the surface, making it much harder to crack or scratch."],
g:[["Silica","A compound of silicon and oxygen, the main part of sand"],["Amorphous","Without a regular crystal structure"],["Glassblowing","Shaping molten glass by blowing air into it through a tube"],["Obsidian","Natural volcanic glass"]],
f:["Glass is made mainly from sand.","Glass can be recycled endlessly.","Lightning striking sand can form natural glass tubes called fulgurites."],
fun:"Old church windows are thicker at the bottom not because glass flows, but because of how glass was made centuries ago.",
tl:"Ang salamin ay gawa sa buhanging tinunaw sa napakataas na temperatura. Mga 4,500 taon nang gumagawa ang tao ng salamin, at ginagamit ito ngayon sa bintana, bote, screen, lente at fiber optic."});

ADDART({id:"salmon",t:"Salmon",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐟",w:"Salmon",r:["fishes","bear","rivers","birdmigr","fish"],
p:[["Fish of two worlds","Salmon hatch in rivers, grow up in the ocean and return to rivers to breed."],["Amazing journeys","They swim hundreds or thousands of kilometers upstream to the stream where they were born."],["Feeding forests","Bears, eagles and even trees depend on salmon runs."]],
l:"Salmon are fish of the family Salmonidae that live in the northern Atlantic and Pacific Oceans and the rivers that flow into them. Most salmon are anadromous: they hatch in freshwater streams, migrate to the ocean to feed and grow, then return, often to the very stream where they were born, to spawn. Their journeys upriver can cover hundreds or even thousands of kilometers, leaping waterfalls and rapids. Pacific salmon die after spawning, while Atlantic salmon may spawn more than once. Salmon runs feed bears, eagles and other wildlife and carry ocean nutrients into forests. Salmon are an important food, and many are now farmed, but wild populations are threatened by dams, overfishing, pollution and warming waters.",
s:[["Life cycle","Salmon eggs are laid in nests, called redds, dug in gravel stream beds. The young hatch as alevins, carrying a yolk sac for food, then become fry and parr, living in the stream for months or years. They then become smolts, which change their bodies to live in salt water, and swim to the sea. After one to several years at sea, adult salmon return to spawn."],["Finding home","Salmon find their way back to their home stream with remarkable accuracy. Scientists think they use Earth’s magnetic field to navigate in the ocean and their strong sense of smell to recognize the unique scent of their home river. As they migrate upstream, many stop eating and their bodies change color and shape."],["Kinds of salmon","Pacific salmon include Chinook, or king, salmon, the largest, which can weigh over 50 kilograms; sockeye, which turn bright red when spawning; and coho, pink and chum salmon. The Atlantic salmon lives in the North Atlantic. Some relatives, such as trout, also migrate, while others live their whole lives in fresh water."],["Salmon and people","Indigenous peoples of the Pacific Northwest have depended on salmon for thousands of years, and it is central to their cultures. Today, most salmon eaten worldwide is farmed in sea cages, especially in Norway and Chile, which raises concerns about disease, parasites and pollution. Removing old dams and restoring rivers has helped some wild runs recover."]],
k:[["Family","Salmonidae"],["Lifestyle","Anadromous: river to sea and back"],["Largest species","Chinook (king) salmon"],["Navigation","Magnetic sense and smell"],["Biggest farmed producers","Norway and Chile"]],
d:[["Thousands of years","Indigenous peoples depend on salmon runs"],["1800s","Dams and overfishing begin to reduce wild salmon"],["1970s","Large-scale salmon farming begins in Norway"],["2011–2014","Elwha River dams removed in the United States"],["2023–24","Klamath River dams removed"]],
s2:[["Bears and forests","When salmon swim upstream, bears catch them and often carry them into the forest to eat. The leftover fish decay and fertilize the soil. Scientists have found that trees near salmon streams can contain nitrogen that came from the ocean, carried there by salmon."],["Removing dams","On the Elwha River in Washington State, two dams that blocked salmon for nearly a century were removed between 2011 and 2014, the largest dam removal in history at the time. Within a few years, salmon returned to stretches of river they had not reached for generations. In 2023–2024, four dams were removed from the Klamath River."]],
sp:["Leaping salmon","Salmon can leap more than 3 meters out of the water to clear waterfalls and rapids on their way upstream. Where dams block their path, people build fish ladders, a series of small pools rising like steps, so the fish can jump from one to the next."],
g:[["Anadromous","Living in the sea but breeding in fresh water"],["Spawn","To lay and fertilize eggs"],["Smolt","A young salmon ready to move to the sea"],["Fish ladder","A series of pools that help fish pass a dam"]],
f:["Pacific salmon die after spawning.","Salmon can leap more than 3 meters.","Salmon use smell to find their home stream."],
fun:"The pink color of salmon flesh comes from pigments in the shrimp and krill they eat.",
tl:"Ang salmon ay isdang napipisa sa ilog, lumalaki sa karagatan at bumabalik sa ilog upang mangitlog. Lumalangoy sila nang daan-daan o libu-libong kilometro pabalik sa sapang pinagmulan nila, at umaasa sa kanila ang mga oso, agila at maging ang kagubatan."});

ADDART({id:"lighthouses",t:"Lighthouses",c:"Technology",i:"🗼",w:"Lighthouse",r:["ships","coasts","egypt","scope","gps"],
p:[["Lights for sailors","Lighthouses are towers with powerful lights that guide ships and warn of danger."],["Ancient wonders","The Lighthouse of Alexandria was one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World."],["Automated today","Most lighthouses now run automatically, without keepers."]],
l:"A lighthouse is a tower or building with a bright light at the top, built on coasts, islands, reefs or harbor entrances to help ships navigate and to warn them of dangerous rocks and shallows. Each lighthouse has its own pattern of flashes, called its characteristic, so sailors can identify it at night, and many are painted with distinctive stripes or colors to be recognized by day. The most famous ancient lighthouse, the Pharos of Alexandria in Egypt, built around 280 BC, was one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World. For centuries, lighthouse keepers tended the lamps. Today most lighthouses are automated, and satellite navigation has reduced their importance, but they remain vital backups and beloved landmarks.",
s:[["Ancient lights","The Pharos of Alexandria, built on an island at the entrance to Alexandria’s harbor, may have been over 100 meters tall, with a fire burning at the top. It stood for over 1,500 years before earthquakes destroyed it. The Tower of Hercules in A Coruña, Spain, built by the Romans in the 1st or 2nd century AD, is the oldest lighthouse still in use."],["Building on rocks","Building lighthouses on wave-swept rocks was a great engineering challenge. In 1759, John Smeaton completed the Eddystone Lighthouse off southwest England using interlocking granite blocks, a design copied for later towers. Some lighthouses were built on iron legs over sandbanks or as floating lightships."],["The Fresnel lens","In 1822, French physicist Augustin-Jean Fresnel invented a lens made of rings of glass prisms that bent light into a powerful beam, far brighter than earlier mirrors. Fresnel lenses allowed lights to be seen 30 kilometers or more out to sea and were installed in lighthouses around the world."],["Keepers and automation","Lighthouse keepers lived in remote places, often with their families, trimming wicks, polishing lenses and winding clockwork that rotated the light, sometimes for years at a time. With electric lights and automatic controls, keepers were gradually replaced; the last British lighthouse was automated in 1998. Many lighthouses are now museums or holiday homes."]],
k:[["Purpose","Guiding ships and warning of danger"],["Ancient wonder","Pharos of Alexandria"],["Oldest still working","Tower of Hercules, Spain"],["Key invention","Fresnel lens, 1822"],["Identification","Each has its own flash pattern"]],
d:[["c.280 BC","The Pharos of Alexandria is built"],["c.AD 100","The Tower of Hercules is built in Spain"],["1759","Smeaton’s Eddystone Lighthouse"],["1822","The Fresnel lens is invented"],["1900s","Electric lights replace oil lamps"],["1998","The last lighthouse in Britain is automated"]],
s2:[["Grace Darling","In 1838, Grace Darling, the daughter of a lighthouse keeper on the Farne Islands off northeast England, rowed out with her father through a storm to rescue survivors of a shipwreck. Their bravery made her a national heroine."],["The word pharos","The Lighthouse of Alexandria was so famous that its name, Pharos, became the word for lighthouse in several languages, such as phare in French and faro in Spanish, Italian and Portuguese."]],
sp:["Reading the lights","Every lighthouse has a characteristic shown on nautical charts, such as one white flash every 10 seconds, or groups of red flashes. By timing the flashes, sailors can tell exactly which lighthouse they are seeing and work out their position."],
g:[["Characteristic","The unique pattern of flashes of a lighthouse"],["Fresnel lens","A lens made of rings of prisms that focuses light into a beam"],["Keeper","A person who looked after a lighthouse"],["Lightship","A ship anchored where a lighthouse cannot be built"]],
f:["The Pharos of Alexandria was one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World.","The Tower of Hercules has guided ships for nearly 2,000 years.","Fresnel lenses can send light over 30 kilometers."],
fun:"Some lighthouses also have foghorns that sound in thick fog, each with its own pattern of blasts.",
tl:"Ang parola ay toreng may malakas na ilaw na gumagabay sa mga barko at nagbababala ng panganib. Isa sa Pitong Kababalaghan ng Sinaunang Mundo ang Parola ng Alexandria, at karamihan sa mga parola ngayon ay awtomatiko na."});
